Gemini said
Since you are already using FFmpeg on Arch, you can definitely reproduce those text overlays. It isn't "complicated" in terms of code, but it does require some precise coordinates.

In FFmpeg, this is done using the drawtext filter. We can stack two filters: one to draw the "boxes" (the backgrounds) and one to draw the text.

The Logic
To match your description, we will define:

Bottom Overlay 1: White background box with Blue text.

Bottom Overlay 2: Blue background box with White text.

The FFmpeg Command
You can run this on your muted_church_video.mp4. Replace the placeholder text with your actual Church details.

Bash
ffmpeg -i muted_church_video.mp4 -vf \
"drawtext=text='FIRST LINE OF TEXT':fontcolor=blue:fontsize=24:box=1:boxcolor=white:x=(w-text_w)/2:y=h-80, \
 drawtext=text='SECOND LINE OF TEXT':fontcolor=white:fontsize=24:box=1:boxcolor=blue:x=(w-text_w)/2:y=h-45" \
-c:a copy final_church_video.mp4
Breaking Down the Syntax
-vf: This starts the "Video Filter" chain.

drawtext: The specific filter for adding text.

x=(w-text_w)/2: This is a standard FFmpeg formula to center the text horizontally.

y=h-80 and y=h-45: This positions the lines near the bottom. h is the height of the video; subtracting a small number moves it up from the very bottom edge.

box=1:boxcolor=...: This creates the colored background rectangle behind your text.

-c:a copy: This time we copy the audio (since you already fixed the mute) and re-encode the video (since we are drawing pixels onto it).

Pro-Tips for a Professional Look
Timing: If you only want these overlays to appear for the first 10 seconds, add :enable='between(t,0,10)' to each drawtext section.

Font: On Arch, FFmpeg usually looks in /usr/share/fonts/. If the text looks blocky, you can specify a path to a better font (like DejaVuSans) by adding :fontfile=/usr/share/fonts/TTF/DejaVuSans.ttf.

Padding: If the colored boxes look too tight around the text, add :boxborderw=10 to the filter to give the text some "breathing room" inside the blue and white bars.

How to test it quickly
Since re-encoding the whole video takes time, run a 5-second test first to see if it looks right:
ffmpeg -i muted_church_video.mp4 -t 5 -vf [COPY_FILTER_ABOVE] test_render.mp4

Once you like the look of test_render.mp4, run the full command and then you'll be ready for that final Replace on Vimeo.

