To trim from 0:00 to 5:00 (removing the first 5 minutes) and keeping everything up to 32:05 (removing everything after that mark), your final clip duration will be exactly 27 minutes and 5 seconds.

The "Clean Cut" Command
Run this in your Arch terminal. We use -c copy so it happens instantly and doesn't touch your pixels:

Bash
ffmpeg -ss 00:05:00 -to 00:32:05 -i original_video.mp4 -c copy trimmed_output.mp4
Why this is the "Safe" way:
Instant Results: Because we aren't re-encoding (-c copy), this should finish in about 5–10 seconds, regardless of the file size.

No Quality Loss: Since we aren't "drawing" anything or changing the resolution, the video remains exactly as vMix recorded it.

No Ghosts: By staying entirely away from the -vf (video filter) flag, there is no way for the four-line font bug to appear.

A Quick Warning on "Keyframes"
When using -c copy, FFmpeg cuts at the nearest "Keyframe" (I-frame).

If your cut at 5:00 feels a half-second "off," it’s because the video had to start at the closest full image.

The Fix: If the timing must be frame-accurate to the millisecond, you have to re-encode (which takes longer):
ffmpeg -ss 00:05:00 -to 00:32:05 -i original_video.mp4 -c:v libx264 -crf 18 -preset ultrafast -c:a copy trimmed_accurate.mp4

How to verify the new length:
Run this to confirm the file is now ~27 minutes long:
ffprobe -v error -show_entries format=duration -of default=noprint_wrappers=1:nokey=1 trimmed_output.mp4

