Gemini said
Since you are dealing with two different resolutions (720p vs 1080p) and two different audio sources (vMix vs iPhone mic), we need a robust filter chain. This command will downscale the 1080p iPhone footage to match the 720p master, pad it to ensure the aspect ratio stays perfect, and normalize the audio so the transition doesn't jump in volume.

The "Universal Match" Command
Bash
ffmpeg -i inputone.mp4 -i inputwo.mp4 -filter_complex \
"[1:v]scale=1280:720:force_original_aspect_ratio=decrease,pad=1280:720:(ow-iw)/2:(oh-ih)/2[v1]; \
 [0:v][v1]concat=n=2:v=1:a=0[vout]; \
 [0:a][1:a]concat=n=2:v=0:a=1,loudnorm=I=-16:TP=-1.5:LRA=11[aout]" \
-map "[vout]" -map "[aout]" -c:v libx264 -crf 18 -preset ultrafast -c:a aac -b:a 192k final_combined_pro.mp4
Why this specific configuration works:
scale=1280:720: This shrinks the 1080p iPhone video to 720p.

pad=1280:720...: iPhone video often has slightly different dimensions or metadata. This ensures that even if the iPhone video was shot vertically or in a different wide-screen ratio, it will fit into the 720p frame with black bars rather than stretching.

loudnorm=I=-16: This is the "secret sauce" for the iPhone mic. It targets an integrated loudness of -16 LUFS (the standard for web/Vimeo), which will bring the iPhone's often-peaky or quiet audio into line with your vMix recording.

-preset ultrafast: Since we are re-encoding everything to unify the streams, this ensures the render finishes as quickly as possible on your machine.

A Quick Arch/FFmpeg Tip:
Because the iPhone records in variable frame rate (VFR) and your vMix is likely constant (CFR), the concat filter with re-encoding is actually safer than the "copy" method. It "flattens" everything to a steady 30fps, preventing the audio from drifting out of sync over time.

