Gemini said
To achieve this in one command, we will use a filter_complex. This allows us to process both videos simultaneously, trimming and muting as needed, before joining them together.

The Single-Command Solution
Bash
ffmpeg -i inputone.mp4 -i inputtwo.mov -filter_complex \
"[0:v]trim=0:01:27:35,setpts=PTS-STARTPTS[v1]; \
 [0:a]atrim=0:01:27:35,asetpts=PTS-STARTPTS[a1]; \
 [1:v]scale=1280:720:force_original_aspect_ratio=decrease,pad=1280:720:(ow-iw)/2:(oh-ih)/2,setpts=PTS-STARTPTS[v2]; \
 [1:a]volume=0,asetpts=PTS-STARTPTS[a2]; \
 [v1][a1][v2][a2]concat=n=2:v=1:a=1[v][a]" \
-map "[v]" -map "[a]" -c:v libx264 -crf 18 -preset ultrafast -c:a aac -b:a 192k final_combined.mp4
How this works (The Filter Breakdown):
Trimming inputone.mp4:

trim=0:01:27:35 (and the corresponding atrim for audio) cuts the video at exactly 1 hour, 27 minutes, and 35 seconds.

setpts=PTS-STARTPTS is necessary to ensure the timestamps reset to zero after the trim.

Preparing inputtwo.mov:

scale=1280:720 and pad work together to force the 1080p source into your 720p master without stretching.

volume=0 effectively mutes the iPhone audio entirely.

Concatenation:

concat=n=2:v=1:a=1 takes the processed video/audio streams from both sources and stitches them together seamlessly.

Important Note on Frame Accuracy:
This command performs a "re-encode" (it is not a -c copy operation). Because it re-encodes, it will be frame-accurate at that 1:27:35 mark. The -preset ultrafast flag is included to keep the processing time as low as possible for your Arch system.

