Gemini said
That is a classic FFmpeg "logic" error. When using the trim filter, if you tell it to trim=0:01:27:35, FFmpeg interprets that as "Keep only the segment from 0 seconds to 1 hour 27 minutes." However, depending on how your specific version of FFmpeg on Arch handles the time string, it may have misinterpreted 0:01:27:35 as "1 minute and 27 seconds" or even "1 second," causing it to drop almost all of your first video.

To make this bulletproof, we will use seconds instead of the HH:MM:SS string and move the trim to the input level (-to). This is much more reliable for large files.

The Corrected "Senior Engineer" Command
We will calculate 1:27:35 as 5255 seconds.

Bash
ffmpeg -i inputone.mp4 -i inputtwo.mov -filter_complex \
"[0:v]trim=duration=5255,setpts=PTS-STARTPTS[v1]; \
 [0:a]atrim=duration=5255,asetpts=PTS-STARTPTS[a1]; \
 [1:v]scale=1280:720:force_original_aspect_ratio=decrease,pad=1280:720:(ow-iw)/2:(oh-ih)/2,setpts=PTS-STARTPTS[v2]; \
 [1:a]volume=0,asetpts=PTS-STARTPTS[a2]; \
 [v1][a1][v2][a2]concat=n=2:v=1:a=1[v][a]" \
-map "[v]" -map "[a]" -c:v libx264 -crf 18 -preset ultrafast -c:a aac -b:a 192k final_combined_v2.mp4
Why this version fixes the "Instant Trim" issue:
trim=duration=5255: By explicitly defining the duration in seconds, we remove any ambiguity about hours vs. minutes.

setpts=PTS-STARTPTS: This is critical. Without this, the second video tries to start at the 1 hour 27 minute mark on the timeline, often resulting in a black screen or a file that "ends" immediately.

volume=0: This ensures your iPhone footage (inputtwo.mov) stays completely silent while the main service audio remains untouched.

A Quick Way to Verify
If you want to test this without waiting for the whole hour to encode, change 5255 to 10 just to see if it correctly joins 10 seconds of the first video to the second video.

